# Navigating Files and Directories

Linux paths use `/` as the separator. The root directory is `/`. A path is
**absolute** when it starts with `/`; otherwise, it is **relative** to the
current directory.

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `pwd` | Print the current directory |
| `ls` | List directory contents |
| `ls -la` | Include hidden files and detailed information |
| `cd /path/to/dir` | Change to a directory |
| `cd ..` | Move to the parent directory |
| `cd ~` | Move to your home directory |

## Common filesystem locations

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `/` | Top of the filesystem |
| `/home` | Users' home directories |
| `/root` | Root user's home directory |
| `/etc` | System-wide configuration |
| `/var` | Frequently changing data, such as logs |
| `/tmp` | Temporary files |
| `/usr` | Many user applications and shared files |
| `/bin` | Essential user commands (often linked to `/usr/bin`) |
